  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🛡️ Security Company Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a guarding firm or alarm installer, from Belt & Braces Security to Bastion Guarding."
    },
    "intro": "Name your firm with this **Security Company Name Generator**: for security guards, door supervisors, event security, and CCTV and alarm installers. Pick a style and the work you do, or add your first name or town.",
    "sections": [],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do I check a security company name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for a security company?",
        "answer": "Customers are trusting you with their homes, staff and events, so the name should sound steady and dependable rather than aggressive. A surname or town with \"Security\" is the classic choice (\"Hartley Security Services\"); strong words like Bastion or animals like Bulldog and Owl make a memorable logo; a light pun (\"Sure Lock Security\") suits an alarm fitter more than a guarding firm. Say what you do if you can: \"Guarding\", \"CCTV\" or \"Alarms\" helps the right customers find you."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I need a licence to use \"Security\" in my business name?",
        "answer": "Not for the word itself in most places, but for the work. In the UK, anyone working as a security guard, door supervisor, CCTV operator (public space surveillance), key holder or close protection officer needs an SIA licence, and firms can join the SIA's Approved Contractor Scheme; alarm and CCTV installers often join NSI or SSAIB. In the US, most states license security guard companies and many license alarm installers, usually under the exact business name you register. Some company registers also check \"Security\" or \"Securities\" because it can read as a finance word. Never use words that suggest the police or the government, such as \"Police\", \"Patrol Authority\", \"Official\", \"National\" or \"Royal\", or claim a licence or approval you don't hold."
      }
    ]
  },
